# Drafted by Gemini (R14 faithful), edited.

~ novice
faithful_novice [smile]: Blessed be the petal, blessed be the thorn, blessed be the hand that prunes the vine! Welcome, pilgrims, mind the wax on the flagstones.
* Tell us about the Crimson Rose's teachings. -> nov_teach
* The bonfires keep this mountain pure. -> nov_praise
* Burning folk alive is monstrous. -> nov_disagree
* [if standing.crimson_faith <= 2 and flag.crimson_devotions >= 3] Make amends -> regain
* What is that great chalice on the altar? -> nov_chalice
* Leave her to her duties. -> END

~ nov_teach
faithful_novice [smile]: The Rose forgives all who bend to the shears, and only the stubborn deadwood goes into the brazier. Father Renathyr says obedience is the sweetest sap.
-> novice

~ nov_praise
faithful_novice [smile]: Oh, you feel the warmth of it too! Father says the smoke rises straight to her garden.
set crimson_devotions += 1
-> novice

~ nov_disagree
set crimson_infractions += 1
standing crimson_faith -1
faithful_novice [afraid]: You mustn't say such things under the red glass. The priors have ears in every arch.
-> novice

~ nov_chalice
faithful_novice [neutral]: I only dust the base with a lambskin cloth before vespers. None of us novices may look inside the bowl.
-> novice

~ regain
faithful_novice [weary]: You have spoken well of the fires lately, though the priors still watch you hard. Will you kneel and mean it?
* [Deception DC 13] Speak with conviction. -> regain_ok | regain_miss
* Step back for now. -> END

~ regain_ok
standing crimson_faith +3
set crimson_devotions = 0
Narrator: Shoulders unknot along the cloister wall, and the whispered tallying of your sins stops for the hour.
faithful_novice [smile]: I knew the thorn would catch in your heart before long. Walk freely, friends.
-> END

~ regain_miss
Narrator: Your words ring like a cracked penny on stone, and the watching faces stay cold.
faithful_novice [neutral]: Keep praying by the pyres, pilgrim. Perhaps the smoke will soften your tongue yet.
-> END

~ cook
Narrator: A heavy smell of tallow and boiled marrow hangs round the copper vat. Sister Edda skims the grey fat without once glancing toward the bolted larder door.
faithful_cook [weary]: Tallow for the tapers, grease for the bell-ropes. Don't stand where the kettle spits.
* What do you keep behind that larder door? -> cook_larder
* [Insight DC 12] She is afraid. -> cook_tell | cook_blind
* Leave the kitchen. -> END

~ cook_larder
faithful_cook [neutral]: Bones for the broth, bone meal for the abbot's rosebeds. Nothing stays wasted up here on the rock.
-> cook

~ cook_tell
Narrator: Her ladle clicks against the copper rim whenever a boot-heel scrapes behind that thick oak door.
faithful_cook [afraid]: The priors' guests take their meals raw in the dark. I only slide the trencher through the slot and run.
-> cook

~ cook_blind
Narrator: She wipes her brow with a greasy apron and gives nothing away but the heat of the stove.
-> cook

~ scribe
faithful_scribe [weary]: By the root we are bound, by the root we are fed, by the root we are bound. If you seek the older ledger on the Holy Vein, check the high scriptorium shelves, my fingers are too cramped to fetch it.
set scribe_slip
-> END

~ cells
Narrator: A row of narrow stone cells smells of wet straw and old iron. Scratchings on the limewash count the three-night intervals between the bells.
-> END

~ parishioners
if standing.crimson_faith >= 3
faithful_novice [smile]: The red glass shines kindly on you tonight, friends. Even Friar Olaf remarked on your reverence.
else
faithful_novice [angry]: You walk these halls with muddy boots and doubtful eyes. State your business in the abbey.
endif
* Exchange a few pleasantries. -> parish_chat
* We are only studying the monastery's ways. -> parish_answer
* [if standing.crimson_faith <= -3] Fight them -> parish_hostile

~ parish_chat
faithful_novice [smile]: May the Night of Flames warm your marrow. Keep to the lighted galleries.
set halls_met
-> END

~ parish_answer
faithful_novice [neutral]: See that you study on your knees when the silver bells ring.
set halls_met
-> END

~ parish_hostile
combat mon_halls_clergy

~ spiked_doors
Narrator: Iron spikes Stud the timber on the hall side, and forcing the latch drives a rusted point straight into {name}'s palm.
damage 1d4 piercing
-> END

~ upper_view
Narrator: From the upper balustrade, the courtyard below looks like a stone well, its charred pyre-post waiting black and crooked in the red lantern light.
-> END
